00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.490
So Grails is an open
source framework

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:01.220
for building web
applications for the JVM.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.100
And it's been around
for over a decade.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:08.780
In February of 2008, is
when we shipped Grails 1.0,

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.380
and the framework predates
1.0 by a year or so.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:16.270
So the framework's been around
for 11 or 12 years, we'll say.

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:19.380
And it's been tremendously
successful over that time.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.050
So we're very
proud of the Grails

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:25.370
framework and the business
that we have built around that.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:28.310
One of the things that
has enabled Grails

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.160
to be as relevant
and as important

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:34.580
as it's been for as long as it
has been, for over a decade,

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.090
is the fact that the technology
has evolved considerably

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.580
since the beginning to
stay relevant to the way

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.200
that folks want to build
applications today.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.150
So in 2007, when we started
all of this certainly

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:48.900
the idea of REST existed.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:51.620
But it was nowhere near as
big of a deal as it is today.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.200
Every enterprise builds
REST services today.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.320
And so in the very
beginning, there

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.180
wasn't a whole lot
of support for REST

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:00.560
in the Grails framework.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.780
You could certainly build
REST services with Grails,

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.947
but there wasn't a whole
lot of support for building

00:08:04.947 --> 00:08:05.930
those kinds of apps.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:09.230
And over the years as REST
got more and more important,

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:12.740
Grails stayed ahead of
that by adapting to the way

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.200
that people want to
build applications.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.710
So over the years, we
built more and more support

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.370
for publishing REST services.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.180
And today we have really, really
terrific support in Grails

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:24.330
for building REST services.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.540
And that's an example of
the technology evolving

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:29.130
over the years to stay relevant.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.870
And that's an important part
of the technology's longevity.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:35.090
GORM falls in that
same category.

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:37.539
GORM is the ORM tool
that's part of Grails

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.039
and it's always
been part of Grails.

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:41.360
It's been part of Grails
since the very beginning.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.840
In the very beginning,
GORM was conceived

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:46.400
to be a layer on
top of Hibernate.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.040
We added a bunch of really
cool and powerful capabilities

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.080
on top of Hibernate
and made it easier

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.370
to take advantage of what
Hibernate had to offer.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.370
But that's what GORM was
in the very beginning.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:59.870
And then over the years, GORM
has evolved to adapt to the way

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.970
that people want to use
the technology like GORM.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.700
So for example, early
on we introduced

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.580
JPA support so you could
pull out Hibernate and plug

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:10.430
into whatever JPA
implementation you like.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:11.900
And then fast
forward a few years

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:14.750
past that and the
so-called noSQL databases

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:16.760
started gaining a lot of
traction and momentum--

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.820
databases like
MongoDB and Cassandra.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:21.590
And there were a
whole bunch of those.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.210
People wanted to
use GORM to talk

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:24.440
to those kinds of database.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:27.470
So we evolved GORM further.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:30.170
And today we've got GORM
implementations for Hibernate

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.680
and JPA and MongoDB
and Neo4j, which

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:36.950
is a really interesting
graph database, Cassandra.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:39.390
We've got a bunch of
GORM implementations.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.810
The point there is
that the technology

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:47.750
has evolved to stay
relevant and to keep itself

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:50.900
an important part of
the landscape in which

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.480
these technologies play.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:56.940
So as we're confronted with
changes in the landscape,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.270
the technology changes
to deal with those.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:02.910
And now today and
in recent years,

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:06.860
one of the changes that we've
had to confront and come up

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:08.420
with solutions for--

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.760
a whole category of challenges--

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:12.300
is microservices.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:15.410
When we first started
building Grails a decade ago,

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:18.740
monoliths were the way that
most folks built and deployed

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:20.180
JVM web applications.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:23.180
You put your whole application
in one big WAR file, drop

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:25.880
that WAR filed in Tomcat or
whatever server container

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:30.182
you prefer, and that's how
you deploy web applications.

00:10:30.182 --> 00:10:31.640
And if any part of
that application

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:34.530
needs to be updated, you have
to update the whole thing.

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:37.020
And there are benefits to
a monolithic architecture

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:37.520
like that.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.520
There are drawbacks to a
monolithic architecture

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:40.380
like that.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.460
But that's how applications,
by and large, were built.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.350
And those are the
kinds of applications

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:48.740
that Grails was initially
designed for and prioritized

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:49.610
for.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.680
Now, as more and
more enterprises

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:53.750
want to build microservice
architectures,

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:55.730
there's a whole
suite of challenges

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:58.520
that come along with building
those kinds of systems.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:03.260
And that's another example
of changes in the landscape

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:06.900
that our technology
needs to adapt to.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.100
So we recognize those
changes, those new challenges.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.500
Google recognizes
those challenges.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.890
And you recognize
those challenges,

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:20.560
or you will as you start
looking into migrating

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:22.720
to microservice architectures.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.860
There are lots of good
reasons to deploy applications

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:29.620
in that kind of architecture,
but all the benefits

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:30.910
don't come for free.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:32.410
There are new
challenges that you'll

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:34.870
have to confront with
microservices that maybe you

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:37.990
didn't have to confront
with monoliths.

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:41.380
And what we're
doing with Micronaut

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:44.050
and our integrations
with GCP is we're

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:47.530
trying to make it as easy as
possible for folks to build

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.350
those kinds of systems and
take advantage of these really

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:53.560
robust services that
Google has to offer

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:57.430
with minimal burden on the
application development team,

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:02.960
and minimal complexity in
the application code base.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:07.060
So we recognized a whole
new set of challenges

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:09.490
that relate to
microservices, and we

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.750
had to decide what
to do about that.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.050
So when we wanted
to evolve GORM,

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:16.420
we realized, hey, we've got
this great tool for dealing

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:17.710
with relational databases.

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:21.520
People want to use
that tool to talk

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:24.010
to non-relational databases,
and we figured out

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:27.046
how to evolve the technology
to deal with that.

00:12:27.046 --> 00:12:30.700
With microservices that
was one of our options.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.860
One of the things we
could have done was

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:35.200
continue to evolve
our current technology

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:40.050
to be more and more
compatible with

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:42.270
microservice architectures.

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:45.190
That that's one approach
we could have taken.

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:47.290
We decided not to
take that approach.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:49.330
Rather than evolve
our current technology

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:52.540
to be a better fit for
microservices, what we decided

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:55.450
to do was design
a whole new thing

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.690
from the ground up specifically
designed for microservices

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:02.050
and specifically prioritizing
microservice architectures.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.760
And that's what Micronaut is.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:07.240
Micronaut is an open
source framework

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:10.570
for building microservice
applications for the JVM.

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:14.740
It's been designed and optimized
specifically for microservices.

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:17.553
So certainly you can build
monoliths with Micronaut

00:13:17.553 --> 00:13:18.970
and people will
do that and people

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:20.480
will have success with that.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:22.480
But we're designing and
optimizing the framework

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.140
specifically for
microservice architectures.

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.280
The runtime is very lightweight.

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:32.530
It's based on Netty, which
offers a bunch of really

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:33.790
positive characteristics.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:34.560
It's really fast.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:35.560
It's really lightweight.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:37.840
It's got a bunch of really
great reactive support

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:39.673
in the framework that
we're taking advantage

00:13:39.673 --> 00:13:41.140
of at the Micronaut level.

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:43.395
So the runtime in Micronaut
is based on Netty.

00:13:43.395 --> 00:13:45.520
And as I said, it's really,
really fast and really,

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:48.100
really lightweight.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:50.440
We'll come back to that
point a little bit later.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:53.750
Micronaut is a
polyglot framework.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:56.620
So the languages that we've got
proper support for in Micronaut

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.010
include Java,
Kotlin, and Groovy.

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:03.430
And all of our annotation
processing that we--

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:06.790
so all the annotations that
we support in Micronaut work

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:08.380
across all three
of those languages.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:09.760
The exact same annotations.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:12.050
They all yield the
same net effects.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:14.823
So if you would prefer to
build your systems with Kotlin

00:14:14.823 --> 00:14:16.990
and you prefer to take
advantage of all the benefits

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:19.030
that Kotlin has
to offer, you can

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:20.890
do that and take
advantage of everything

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:22.520
that Micronaut has to offer.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.030
If you prefer dynamic
languages like Groovy,

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:26.920
we've got terrific
support for doing that.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:28.420
If you're a Java
shop and don't want

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:31.480
to introduce any of these
other non-Java JVM languages,

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:33.880
you can build your
whole systems with Java.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:37.490
And the languages can mix
and match within a project.

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:40.310
So within a single
Micronaut service,

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:42.570
you can have Java code and
Kotlin code and Groovy code

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:46.340
and it all just plays well
together really, really well.

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.500
So it's a polyglot
framework for the JVM.

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:52.570
Micronaut is really
not just one thing.

00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:55.030
It's really a whole
portfolio of things.

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:57.580
And that portfolio of things
includes some components

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.620
like an aspect
oriented programming

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.810
engine, and a
dependency injection

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.450
container, and an HTTP client.

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:07.570
And a number of these
components can be used separate

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:08.740
from the rest of Micronaut.

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:10.990
So for example, our
dependency injection container

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:13.720
that we've built in Micronaut
has some really interesting

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:16.750
innovative characteristics
to it that I'll talk about.

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:19.390
But that DI container
is usable separate

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:21.140
from the rest of Micronaut.

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:25.240
So if you're building
a Java application

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.010
and you want a DI
container, you can

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:30.430
use Micronaut's DI container
in that job application

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:32.860
without using any of
our HTTP server stuff,

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:35.980
or without using any of the
other components that are part

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:40.400
of Micronaut, which is great.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:42.190
Some of the interesting
characteristics

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:44.350
of our dependency
injection container

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:48.430
all have to do with the fact
that our DI container doesn't

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:52.060
do any runtime reflection,
which, it turns out,

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:53.720
is a really big deal.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:57.970
One of the reasons that our
current technology before we

00:15:57.970 --> 00:15:59.310
built Micronaut--

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:02.440
so Grails in particular-- one of
the reasons that Grails is not

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:05.560
a terrific fit for
building microservices

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:08.810
has to do with Grails dependence
on the Spring framework.

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:10.430
And the Spring
framework is terrific.

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:12.388
There's all kinds of
really, really great stuff

00:16:12.388 --> 00:16:14.890
that Spring does, and
those are the reasons

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:18.260
that we built Grails on
the Spring framework.

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.480
But Spring is
expensive in some ways,

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:21.950
for certain kinds
of applications.

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:24.200
And a lot of that has to
do with Spring's dependence

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:27.565
on runtime reflection
and dynamic proxies.

00:16:27.565 --> 00:16:28.940
So what we've done
with Micronaut

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:32.150
is we've eliminated all the
runtime reflection and all

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:35.090
the runtime proxies
and replaced that

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:39.290
with a really sophisticated,
ahead of time compilation

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:42.770
compile time tricks to do all
the stuff that we otherwise

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:45.240
would have done with
reflection at runtime.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:47.150
So all of our
annotation processing

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.130
is all configured
at compile time.

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:50.840
Our whole dependency
injection container

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:52.790
is all configured
at compile time.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:56.120
No runtime proxies,
no runtime reflection.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.610
And that is where a lot of
our performance benefits

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:01.070
are coming from in
Micronaut and where

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:04.819
a lot of other positive aspects
like much smaller memory

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:06.240
footprints.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:09.950
So we'll talk a little bit
more about that as we press on.

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:12.859
As I said, Micronaut is
being designed and optimized

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:15.619
specifically for
building microservices.

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:17.817
And in a microservice
architecture,

00:17:17.817 --> 00:17:20.359
you don't have one thing, you
have numerous things, and maybe

00:17:20.359 --> 00:17:21.430
lots of things.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:23.980
You have this whole
architecture of components

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.230
communicating with each other.

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:27.500
So a request comes into the
system and hits component

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:29.600
A, and component A
calls component B,

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:31.722
and component B
calls component C.

00:17:31.722 --> 00:17:33.680
And that's how these
microservice architectures

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:34.340
work.

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:35.840
In each one of
those interactions,

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:37.490
you've got a client
and a server.

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:41.130
When A calls B, A is a
client and B is a server.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:43.970
And when B calls C, B
becomes a client and C

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:47.120
is the server for that
particular communication.

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:50.000
Because that's the way
microserver architectures work,

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.150
Micronaut prioritizes both
sides of those communications.

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:56.223
So we've got a really, really
sophisticated HTTP client,

00:17:56.223 --> 00:17:58.640
as well as a bunch of really
cool and powerful server side

00:17:58.640 --> 00:17:59.140
stuff.

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:02.840
And we'll look at
examples of both of those.

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:06.320
Some of the priorities
that the framework has

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:09.620
include things like
developer productivity.

00:18:09.620 --> 00:18:12.230
Time to market is really
important and really valuable,

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:13.310
and we recognize that.

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:16.640
So we make a lot of design
decisions specifically targeted

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:21.110
at contributing to
developer productivity.

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:23.450
And another attribute
that goes along with that

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:24.745
is code simplicity.

00:18:24.745 --> 00:18:26.120
In a Micronaut
application you're

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:28.037
going to write considerably
less code than you

00:18:28.037 --> 00:18:30.090
might with other frameworks.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:34.970
And all that is related to
not only time to market,

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:38.340
but ease of maintaining and
extending the system over time.

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:41.900
So we prioritize developer
productivity and code

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:46.560
simplicity while embracing a
familiar programming model.

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:48.680
So folks who are used
to building applications

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.960
with modern JVM
web frameworks will

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:53.600
see a lot of the
same kinds of idioms

00:18:53.600 --> 00:18:55.440
show up in a
Micronaut application.

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:57.830
So for example, you can
write a service class

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:01.340
and annotate that service
class with at transactional

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:04.160
and a bunch of really cool and
powerful transaction management

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.480
related stuff will be
associated with that class.

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:11.760
What I just now described isn't
new, and isn't innovative.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:14.090
Those capabilities have
been around for a long time.

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:16.280
But how we're doing that
is new and innovative.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:19.460
And again, that has to do
with all of our compile time

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:21.470
annotation processing
stuff, which

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.500
allows us to rig up things
like the DI container

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:27.740
all at compile time and not
rely on any runtime reflection

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:29.780
or runtime practices.

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:31.580
And a particularly
important part

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:34.790
of all of this that we
want to focus on today

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:39.290
is our ease of integration
with these really, really

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:43.050
sophisticated enterprise
scale type cloud services.

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:45.500
So if you're going to build
a microservice architecture

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.960
and deploy it to
GCP, you're probably

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:52.140
going to want to take advantage
of things like CloudSQL

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:56.210
and Kubernetes and all
of these robust services

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:59.480
that Google has provided.

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:01.520
One of the things that
Micronaut is doing

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:04.430
is making it very, very
easy to take advantage

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:07.730
of those services without having
to take on much of the burden

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:09.650
or the cost associated with--

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:11.120
the development
burdens associated

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:15.450
with building systems that take
advantage of those services.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:17.720
And the framework is
really, really fast.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:20.180
Runtime performance
is another one

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:23.310
of the things that's an
important attribute that

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.770
effects all of our
design decisions.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:28.100
So we want you to be able
to build the thing quickly.

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:31.520
We want it to be a simple,
easy to maintain, and easy

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:32.840
to extend code.

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:34.490
We want you to be
able to integrate

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:39.170
with these robust suite of cloud
services that are out there.

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:42.960
And we need the thing to be
really, really fast at runtime.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:45.020
And in fact, it is.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:46.600
So some of the
important benefits

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:51.080
that Micronaut has to offer
include runtime performance

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:55.220
that shows up in terms of
startup time for your services,

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:58.500
as well as performance while
the application is running.

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:02.660
So a typical Micronaut service
can be started up in something

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:03.813
like a second.

00:21:03.813 --> 00:21:06.230
Sometimes less than a second,
sometimes a little bit more.

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:08.510
It depends on what the
application is doing.

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:10.670
But a second puts you
in the neighborhood.

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:14.130
It's not like 20 seconds or
30 seconds or even 10 seconds.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:17.290
One second startup time
puts you in the ballpark.

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:18.640
Minimal memory requirements.

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:20.740
So a small Micronaut
service can run

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.265
in about 10 megabytes
of heap, and depending

00:21:23.265 --> 00:21:24.640
on what your
application is doing

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:26.770
will dictate how much
above that you need.

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:29.260
But that kind of
represents the overhead

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:32.390
or the tax associated
with the framework itself,

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:35.488
which is really,
really quite small.

00:21:35.488 --> 00:21:37.780
We're not going to look a
whole lot of code here today,

00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:40.060
but we'll see a
little bit of code.

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:44.740
But one of the attributes of
systems built with Micronaut

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:47.260
is the code really
is quite simple.

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:49.840
As I said before, we're
embracing familiar programming

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:54.700
models and enabling
easy integration

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:57.090
with Cloud Services.

00:21:57.090 --> 00:22:02.007
So a small Micronaut JAR file
might be about 8 megabytes.

00:22:02.007 --> 00:22:03.340
That's the size of the JAR file.

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:05.110
And of course, the more code
you put in your project,

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:06.830
the JAR profile is
going to get bigger.

00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:09.010
But that, again,
represents the overhead

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:11.800
that the framework brings along
which is really quite small.

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:14.992
And you see the startup time and
heap requirement numbers there.

00:22:14.992 --> 00:22:17.200
And I want to take just a
minute to talk a little bit

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:22.030
about these are attributes that
relate to microservices in ways

00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:23.620
that they don't
necessarily relate

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:25.240
to monolithic applications.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:28.550
So think about memory
consumption as an example.

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:30.970
If you're building a
monolithic application

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:33.520
and that application
requires a gig of RAM

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:36.010
to run, in your
development environment

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:39.280
you start the thing
up with Gradle

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:41.830
or whatever tool you're
using to start the app.

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:43.720
And you allocate a gig
of RAM to the thing,

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:45.845
or maybe the startup scripts
are doing that for you

00:22:45.845 --> 00:22:47.800
and you don't even
really think about it.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:51.760
But allocating a gig of
RAM to your monolithic app

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:54.460
may not be that big of a
deal because there's only one

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:55.240
of them.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:56.890
In a microservice
architecture, there's

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:58.057
not going to be one of them.

00:22:58.057 --> 00:23:00.050
There's going to be
maybe lots of them.

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:06.230
So if you've got a monolithic
servlet container based web app

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:10.420
and try to start, say 30
of those up concurrently

00:23:10.420 --> 00:23:11.680
on your development machine.

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:14.150
And it's just not going to
work for a number of reasons.

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:15.817
And one of those
reasons is you're going

00:23:15.817 --> 00:23:18.130
to run into memory issues.

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:19.802
In a microservice
architecture, you

00:23:19.802 --> 00:23:21.010
might really want to do that.

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:23.320
You might want to start up
a whole bunch of services

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:26.570
on one machine in your
development environment.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:31.360
So the memory requirements,
they factor into that equation

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:35.560
differently than they would
for a monolithic application.

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:37.000
And in your
deployment environment

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.580
on the cloud where you're
paying for VMs that

00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:42.610
based on how many CPUs used and
how much RAM you're allocating

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:47.140
to them, adding up all of
those large memory requirement

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:50.540
machines in your cloud
environment can get expensive.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:52.660
So these are examples
of attributes

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:57.010
that they affect your
microservice architectures

00:23:57.010 --> 00:24:03.430
differently than they affect
monolithic applications.

00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:07.600
So one of the things
that Micronaut does

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:10.750
is it makes it very, very
easy to take advantage

00:24:10.750 --> 00:24:14.830
of these robust cloud services
that the cloud providers make

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:15.980
available.

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:17.770
So we've got really,
really great support

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:20.720
for service discovery
by way of Consul.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:21.940
We've got support for Eureka.

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:24.490
We've got support
for Kubernetes.

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:28.750
So we support all the major
service discovery engines.

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:31.450
And again, this is an
example of a thing that's

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:34.210
important in a microservice
application that's

00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:36.790
not important, or maybe
not even relevant at all

00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:39.460
in the context of a
monolithic application.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:42.113
In a monolith there's only
one, so there is no discovery.

00:24:42.113 --> 00:24:44.530
You're not going out and trying
to find these other things

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:45.860
to communicate with.

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:49.330
So service discovery is
an example of a capability

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:50.800
that the framework
provides to make

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:52.810
it easy to take
advantage of something

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:55.120
like service
discovery that is part

00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:58.200
of that new suite of challenges
that microservice architectures

00:24:58.200 --> 00:24:59.710
bring along.

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:01.710
Configuration sharing is
kind of the same thing.

00:25:01.710 --> 00:25:03.627
If you have lots of
microservices deployed out

00:25:03.627 --> 00:25:05.265
in your cloud
environment, you probably

00:25:05.265 --> 00:25:06.640
want them to share
configuration.

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:09.100
And we've built really,
really sophisticated systems

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:10.810
to make it easy
to take advantage

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:15.070
of distributed configuration
engines and all the major cloud

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:16.210
providers.

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:18.430
We've got some really
interesting client side load

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:20.710
balancing stuff built
into our HTTP client,

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:22.990
that if I've got some time
I'll be able to demonstrate

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:23.782
a little bit later.

00:25:23.782 --> 00:25:26.890
We've got support for building
message driven applications.

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:30.280
We've got integrations with
technologies like Kafka.

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:32.950
We've got really great support
for serverless computing,

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:34.520
and a lot more.

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:38.410
So Micronaut is not
just a framework

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:40.360
for building REST services.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:42.250
It's not just that.

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:44.415
It is that, but it's a
whole bunch more than that.

00:25:44.415 --> 00:25:46.540
And a lot of that has to
do with all of these cloud

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:48.790
native capabilities
that we've built right

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:50.620
into the technology.

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:53.350
This quote here that
was posted on Twitter--

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:55.240
I believe this was
posted before we even

00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:59.960
got to release 1.0, which we
released this past October--

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:03.700
this person recognized
something that--

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:05.290
what he's recognizing
is that one

00:26:05.290 --> 00:26:07.780
of the things that
Micronaut is and does

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:10.930
is it enables you to very easily
build these systems to take

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:13.820
advantage of all these
services like Kafka

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:16.330
and Consul, and Zipkin,
and MongoDB, and JPA,

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:16.925
and on and on.

00:26:16.925 --> 00:26:18.550
That's one of the
things that Micronaut

00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:22.900
is, is it's an
accelerator for folks

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:26.500
who want to take advantage of
these robust cloud services

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:31.030
without having to spend lots
of time and development effort

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:35.680
building the integrations
with those systems.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:40.810
So Google has been a
priority for the Micronaut

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:43.330
team at Object Computing really
pretty much the whole time

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:44.663
that we've been working on this.

00:26:44.663 --> 00:26:47.450
But certainly right now
Google is a priority.

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:48.640
We've partnered with Google.

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:51.830
Object Computing as an
official Google partner.

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:54.490
And we've got really
great support in Micronaut

00:26:54.490 --> 00:27:00.250
for taking advantage of
GCP Google Cloud Services.

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:02.710
So we've got support for
Kubernetes, and Stackdriver,

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:05.300
and gRPC, and there are others.

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:08.140
Some of these, Google's
actually contributed the support

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:10.000
to the framework.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:14.470
So for example, this week we'll
be releasing Micronaut 1.1.

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:17.290
And in 1.1 we've
got some new support

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:19.750
for Stackdriver and gRPC.

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:21.580
And some of that was
developed by Google,

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:25.810
and they contributed those
efforts to the open source

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:28.240
software, which is fantastic.

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:31.210
And that's a little bit of
putting your money where

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:34.120
your mouth is with respect to
what Google said this morning

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:35.200
during the keynote.

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:37.930
Google says that they're
committed to open source,

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:39.970
and Google really is
committed to open source.

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:45.170
And this is one small
way that that manifests.

00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:46.840
All right.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:49.440
So I want to take a look
at a little bit of code,

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.500
and that'll help us
tease out some things

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:54.970
that I think you'll
find interesting.

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:59.880
So this could be a Java
class in a Micronaut service.

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:01.523
And we've got a controller.

00:28:01.523 --> 00:28:02.940
What makes this
thing a controller

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:05.297
is it's annotated
with App Controller.

00:28:05.297 --> 00:28:07.380
Those of you who might be
familiar with the Grails

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:10.080
framework might recognize
that in Grails we

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:12.690
have lots of
conventions in place

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:15.150
that do things like if you've
got a class whose name ends

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:18.180
in the word controller
it becomes a controller.

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.160
And those conventions
do not exist

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:21.400
in the Micronaut framework.

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:23.880
So what makes this a controller
is not the class name.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:25.470
The class name
could be anything.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:27.360
What makes it a controller
is it's annotated

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:28.860
with that controller.

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:30.810
And there's a method in
this controller that's

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:32.040
annotated with that at get.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:35.430
So if I send and get requests
to slash hello, slash something

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:37.360
this controller
action gets executed.

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:39.370
And then I can do
whatever I need to do.

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:41.298
And there's a lot I can
do in this controller.

00:28:41.298 --> 00:28:43.590
There's a bunch of interesting
data marshaling going on

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:45.420
in both directions for
reading and writing

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:48.380
the body of the request
and the response.

00:28:48.380 --> 00:28:50.180
There's a bunch
of reactive stuff.

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:52.138
There's a whole lot you
can do on a controller.

00:28:52.138 --> 00:28:56.610
This example is literally a
whole world kind of example.

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:58.860
But that's a
controller that could

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.440
be in a Micronaut service.

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:04.500
And in fact, the service
that I was interacting

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:06.500
with from the command
line at the very beginning

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:09.180
of the session looked
a whole lot like that.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:11.280
So we've got this
backend application

00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:13.010
that has a controller in it.

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:14.640
Then I might have
another service.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:16.680
This is not in the
same application.

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:19.200
This is a whole
different service

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:21.970
that's part of our
microservice architecture.

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:23.393
This is the frontend.

00:29:23.393 --> 00:29:24.810
So I've got a
backend piece that's

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:27.300
maybe talking to a database
and doing whatever it's doing.

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:29.508
And separate from that, I've
got another microservice

00:29:29.508 --> 00:29:31.540
that is in the frontend.

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:36.068
And this thing wants to
call the backend service.

00:29:36.068 --> 00:29:37.860
That's how microservice
architectures work.

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:40.870
You've got these components,
making calls to each other.

00:29:40.870 --> 00:29:45.570
This is one microservice
making a call to another.

00:29:45.570 --> 00:29:47.970
And the call is happening at
that last line of code there

00:29:47.970 --> 00:29:51.540
where it says,
BackendClient.hello(name).

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:55.190
That's actually making an HTTP
call to some other service.

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:57.330
There are a number of
interesting things going on

00:29:57.330 --> 00:29:58.830
in that method call,
and we're going

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:01.080
to be able to press into
just a little bit of that.

00:30:01.080 --> 00:30:03.960
Notice that BackendClient that
we're interacting with there

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:05.910
was injected into
this controller

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:08.500
by way of dependency injection.

00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:11.490
And so we've got
a constructor here

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:14.250
that accepts a backend
client as a parameter.

00:30:14.250 --> 00:30:16.350
And at compile
time, Micronaut will

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:18.415
recognize that that
constructor is there

00:30:18.415 --> 00:30:20.040
and create all the
instructions that it

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:22.830
takes to not only
create this thing,

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:25.650
but to pass the required
dependencies as constructor

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:28.540
parameters when it is created.

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:31.890
So the BackendClient is being
injected into this controller,

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:34.270
and that's one of the
things I want to look at.

00:30:34.270 --> 00:30:39.190
And this is the BackendClient,
and this looks terribly benign.

00:30:39.190 --> 00:30:40.688
There's really
nothing interesting

00:30:40.688 --> 00:30:42.730
that's apparent from
looking at that source code.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:44.190
It's not even a class.

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:46.870
It's just an interface
that has a method in it.

00:30:46.870 --> 00:30:49.260
And there are a
couple of annotations.

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:51.750
As uninteresting as
the source code looks,

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:54.670
what's actually going
on is quite interesting.

00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:57.150
So at compile time what
the framework is doing

00:30:57.150 --> 00:30:58.830
is creating an instance--

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:01.440
creating a class that
implements this interface.

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:04.410
And in that class will
be a method called hello.

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:07.500
And when you call that
method, an HTTP request

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:09.900
will be made to some
remote service--

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:12.510
and we'll figure out
how we know about where

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:14.170
that remote services
before we're done.

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:18.690
But we're making an HTTP
call to some remote service

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:20.400
and passing--

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:23.590
so if I call hello and
pass Jeff as a parameter,

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:26.670
then the request will be sent
to slash hello, slash Jeff.

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:29.440
So we've made it very, very
easy to make an HTTP call.

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:31.690
But this is more than just that.

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:35.160
So when I annotated this
interface with at client,

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:37.830
that's what makes this
thing and HTTP client.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:39.810
And the value that
I can specify there

00:31:39.810 --> 00:31:41.920
can be one of a
number of things.

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:45.180
So where you see BackendURL
inside of the curly braces,

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:46.780
that's a variable.

00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:48.390
It's a config variable.

00:31:48.390 --> 00:31:51.730
So whatever the value
of Backend.URL is,

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:53.380
that'll be inserted
at this point.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:55.140
So instead of using
a config variable,

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:56.380
I can put a URL there.

00:31:56.380 --> 00:32:00.190
I can put and HTTPS://
and have a URL there.

00:32:00.190 --> 00:32:02.040
And now when I make
calls to this method,

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:05.280
that URL represents where the
remote service is that I'll

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.140
be communicating with.

00:32:08.140 --> 00:32:10.560
And there are other values
that that BackendURL can have.

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:13.630
But let's press on here.

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:15.780
So now I've got
some configuration

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:20.010
in the backend and the
frontend represented here.

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:21.780
Remember these are
two separate services,

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:23.880
two separate
applications we'll say.

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:25.560
In my development
environment I want

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:27.480
to be able to run these
things concurrently.

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:30.090
I want to be able to run the
backend and run the frontend.

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:33.850
And because of that they need
to run on different ports.

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:36.690
So in my backend
config file, I've

00:32:36.690 --> 00:32:39.330
configured the thing
to run on port 8082.

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:41.760
In the frontend config
file, I haven't specified

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:43.630
what port I wanted to run on.

00:32:43.630 --> 00:32:45.860
So by default it'll
run on port 8080.

00:32:45.860 --> 00:32:49.160
So now when I start these things
up, the backend is on 8082.

00:32:49.160 --> 00:32:54.320
The frontend is on 8080, and
the value of backend.URL is what

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:55.790
you see in this config file--

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:57.690
localhost 8082.

00:32:57.690 --> 00:33:00.980
So the config file on the right
is in the frontend project.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:03.590
And that's the frontend
project being told

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:05.090
where the backend service is.

00:33:05.090 --> 00:33:08.600
The backend service
is on localhost 8082.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:11.760
And that works fine in my
development environment.

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:16.610
But what about when
I deploy this to GCP?

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:18.200
My backend service
is not going to be

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:20.570
running on local host 8082.

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:24.170
It's going to be
running someplace else.

00:33:24.170 --> 00:33:25.670
So these are the
config files that I

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:28.100
would have in my project
that support my development

00:33:28.100 --> 00:33:29.520
environment.

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:32.480
In addition to those
application .yml files,

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:38.030
you can have config files with
names like application-GCP.yml.

00:33:38.030 --> 00:33:42.200
Now that -GCP means something
special to Micronaut.

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:44.450
So these config
files will only be

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:48.110
loaded if the application
is running on GCP.

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:51.050
So if I run these applications
in my development environment,

00:33:51.050 --> 00:33:53.090
these configure are in place.

00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:57.740
When I build those applications
and deploy them to GCP,

00:33:57.740 --> 00:34:00.260
these configs are
in place in addition

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:04.010
to the application.yml
configs, and these settings

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:05.430
take precedence.

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:10.760
So in the backend service, I've
got application-GCP configured

00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:13.670
so the service runs
on port 8080, Which

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:16.219
would be the default
value had I not changed

00:34:16.219 --> 00:34:18.810
that in the config
file there on the left.

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:21.560
Remember I set that to 8020 for
the benefit of my development

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:22.460
environment.

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:25.730
When I deploy to GCP,
I want that service

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:29.580
to run on port 8080 so I've
got a supporting config there.

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:31.310
And likewise in
the frontend, when

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:35.179
I'm running on GCP that's where
the backend service is running

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:36.659
is that URL that you see there.

00:34:36.659 --> 00:34:39.290
So instead of looking
at local host 8082,

00:34:39.290 --> 00:34:41.670
we're looking at the
URL you see there.

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:43.909
That's where the frontend
will be making calls

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:47.179
to to get data from the backend.

00:34:47.179 --> 00:34:51.380
And this is one small example
of support in the framework

00:34:51.380 --> 00:34:54.350
specifically targeted at GCP.

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:58.040
And here I'm just configuring
a port number and a URL.

00:34:58.040 --> 00:34:59.810
But all of your
application config--

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:02.000
any of your
application config that

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:06.400
needs to be different in
the cloud environment,

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:08.690
GCP cloud environment
in particular,

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:13.850
we provided a place for
you to put those configs.

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:17.270
So now I'm ready to publish
these applications to GCP.

00:35:17.270 --> 00:35:19.900
And so I've got a
couple of app.yml files.

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:21.650
The URL down there at
the bottom gives you

00:35:21.650 --> 00:35:23.390
the full reference
for all the things you

00:35:23.390 --> 00:35:25.700
can do in these app.yml files.

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:28.500
I've kept these
really, really simple.

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:30.578
So on the left is
the backend service.

00:35:30.578 --> 00:35:32.120
On the right is the
frontend service,

