00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:34.580
But we've made it very, very
easy to deploy applications

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:36.530
to GCP.

00:37:36.530 --> 00:37:39.800
One more example of
GCP specific config

00:37:39.800 --> 00:37:42.500
is in addition to
application.yml,

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:44.240
you can express your
Micronaut config

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:46.980
in an executable Groovy code.

00:37:46.980 --> 00:37:49.920
And now you can have arbitrary
logic in your config file

00:37:49.920 --> 00:37:52.090
for generating config values.

00:37:52.090 --> 00:37:55.920
And in particular case, reaching
out to Google's metadata

00:37:55.920 --> 00:37:59.880
server to grab some
secrets about my database

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:02.950
URL, my database user
name, my database password.

00:38:02.950 --> 00:38:06.205
So this is code that I can
commit to source code control.

00:38:06.205 --> 00:38:07.080
There's no passwords.

00:38:07.080 --> 00:38:08.520
There's no secrets here.

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:11.190
This code only gets evaluated
when the application's

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:12.870
running on GCP.

00:38:12.870 --> 00:38:14.940
And when the application
is running on GCP,

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:16.800
I'm getting all my
database credentials

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:19.560
from the metadata server there.

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:22.720
You've got a lot of
flexibility there.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:27.600
Micronaut is great
really as a component

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:31.210
of many parts of your
application architecture.

00:38:31.210 --> 00:38:33.750
So the easy one to think
about is microservices.

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:36.330
People build microservices,
they deploy them to GCP,

